# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
  "base": [
    ("Base Depth for a Paver Deck Follows the Patio Spec, Not the Driveway Spec",
     "<p>A paver pool deck carries the same light pedestrian load a patio does, lounge chairs, foot traffic, the occasional umbrella stand, so the ICPI minimum of 4 inches of compacted base on well-drained soil applies here too, not the heavier 6-inch driveway spec. What's different around a pool is how much more moisture the base near the water's edge sees from splash-out, backwash and equipment pad runoff, on top of whatever rain the rest of the yard gets. On a {city} job, we treat the few feet closest to the coping as wetter ground by default and compact accordingly, even on a lot where the rest of the base tests dry.</p>"),
    ("Grading a Paver Deck to the Drains, Not Just Away From the House",
     "<p>Like any pool deck, a paver field around the water needs to shed rain toward deck drains or a perimeter channel instead of back into the pool, on top of the ordinary slope away from the house any patio needs. We build that grade into the compacted base itself, shaping it before the bedding sand goes down, since correcting a pitch problem after pavers are laid means pulling up a section rather than adjusting a sand layer. On a {city} deck, getting that two-direction slope right in the base is what keeps a hard afternoon rain from pushing pollen and yard debris straight into the pool.</p>"),
    ("Where the Screen Cage Footer Lands in a Paver Field",
     "<p>A screen enclosure's aluminum posts typically sit on isolated concrete footers poured below the compacted base, the same as they would on a paver patio, but the stakes are higher around a pool since the posts often sit close to the coping line. We lay out footer locations before compacting the surrounding base and leave the paver courses free to be lifted and reset around them without disturbing the footer itself. On a {city} job, coordinating that layout with the screen contractor ahead of the paver install avoids cutting a finished base to make room for a footer nobody planned for.</p>"),
    ("Flatwoods Soil and the Extra Base a Wet Pool Area Needs",
     "<p>Flatwoods soils like Myakka and Smyrna hold a water table within 18 inches of the surface for part of the year across much of this territory, and a pool deck sits right where splash-out and equipment runoff add even more moisture to ground that's already seasonally wet. ICPI calls for 2 to 4 inches of extra base thickness on continually wet or weak soil, and around a {city} pool we apply that standard to the paver field as a default rather than waiting to see which spots test wet first. A perforated drain line through the thicker section helps where the soil genuinely can't shed water on its own.</p>"),
    ("Ridge Sand Doesn't Mean a Thinner Base Near the Pool Either",
     "<p>On the excessively drained ridge sands west of Orlando, in series like Candler and Astatula, water moves through the ground so fast that a paver base can seem like overkill next to a pool that's constantly adding its own water through splash-out. It isn't. Loose, fast-draining sand shifts under foot traffic and pool furniture more than a firmer soil does, regardless of how well it drains, so a {city} deck on ridge sand still gets the full compacted base depth rather than a thin leveling layer over native material. The water table sitting deep doesn't change how the sand behaves under repeated weight.</p>"),
    ("Setting Pavers Directly Over an Existing Concrete Deck",
     "<p>Setting pavers over an existing concrete pool deck skips the excavation and compacted aggregate a ground-up install needs, but it isn't a shortcut on prep. We sound the old slab for soft or hollow spots with a hammer test, patch anything that fails, and clean the surface before a thin bedding layer, either a sand-set system on a membrane or a mortar-set bed, goes down to receive the pavers. On a {city} overlay job, the old slab has to be structurally sound and draining correctly on its own, since an overlay doesn't fix a base problem underneath it, it just covers it over.</p>"),
    ("Coping Sits on a Different Base Than the Field Pavers",
     "<p>Where the field pavers around a pool are sand-set on compacted aggregate, the coping pieces along the water's edge are almost always mortar-set directly onto the pool's bond beam or a concrete collar built for that purpose, a different structural system entirely. We build that transition so the mortar-set coping and the sand-set field meet cleanly at the same elevation, with the field's base stopping short of the bond beam rather than running underneath the coping. On a {city} deck, mixing up which material belongs on which base is one of the more common reasons a coping line ends up uneven against the paver field next to it.</p>"),
    ("Downspout and Equipment Pad Runoff Eroding the Paver Base",
     "<p>A gutter downspout or the condensate line off a pool heater dumping onto the same stretch of paver base, storm after storm, washes fines out from underneath the nearest pavers faster than ordinary rain does anywhere else on the deck. We route that discharge away from the paver field or under it through solid pipe before the base goes in, rather than leaving it to erode a channel into finished aggregate later. On a {city} deck where the equipment pad sits close to the paver field, that single detail prevents the kind of slow, localized settling that otherwise shows up first as a soft spot next to the equipment.</p>"),
  ],
  "build": [
    ("Paver Thickness for a Pool Deck Matches a Patio, Not a Driveway",
     "<p>ICPI's 60 mm, 2.375-inch standard for pedestrian areas covers a pool deck the same way it covers a patio, since lounge chairs and bare feet don't come close to the load a driveway carries. A {city} homeowner who's seen 80 mm units quoted for a neighbor's driveway doesn't need that heavier paver around a pool, where the extra thickness adds cost without adding anything the job actually requires. What matters more for a paver deck's long-term feel is a properly compacted base and a tight joint at every edge, including the one against the coping, not a thicker unit sitting on an otherwise ordinary base.</p>"),
    ("Travertine, Shell Stone or Concrete Pavers: What Changes Around a Pool",
     "<p>Travertine stays noticeably cooler underfoot in direct sun than a dark concrete paver, part of why it's a common choice right around a {city} pool, though we won't put a number on that difference since no verified measurement exists for Florida conditions. Shell stone, quarried coquina with visible shell fragments, offers a similarly light, naturally textured surface with its own look. Both are more porous than concrete and need sealing attention sooner. Concrete pavers cost less, come in more colors, and are cheaper to replace individually if one cracks, trading some of that heat and texture advantage for lower material cost.</p>"),
    ("The Coping Joint Needs a Sealant, Not Just Sand",
     "<p>Where the field pavers meet mortar-set coping at the pool's edge, a standard sand joint isn't the right detail, since the two systems move independently and a rigid mortar line right there cracks the same way a concrete deck would without an isolation joint. We detail that transition with a flexible sealant over a backer rod instead, the same approach a poured concrete deck uses at its own coping line. On a {city} paver deck, butting sand-set pavers straight against mortar-set coping without that flexible joint is one of the more common reasons the first paver course against the pool starts rocking within a year or two.</p>"),
    ("Bullnose Coping Units and Why the Edge Profile Matters",
     "<p>Precast bullnose coping, a paver unit with a rounded, overhanging front edge, is the standard detail where a paver field meets the water, since a square-edged unit left proud of the pool's bond beam is uncomfortable against bare skin and catches toes climbing out. Bullnose units typically price separately from field pavers, with published ranges running $20 to $40 per linear foot. On a {city} deck, we set the coping course first and work the field pattern back from that fixed line, rather than fitting coping in last against whatever field layout was already set, which is how coping joints end up uneven.</p>"),
    ("Edge Restraint Where the Paver Field Meets the Coping",
     "<p>Every paver field needs a continuous edge restraint, and on a pool deck that includes the inside edge where the field meets the coping, not just the outer perimeter against turf or mulch. The restraint spikes into the compacted base, not into the thin bedding layer under the coping itself, so the two systems stay independently supported. On a {city} deck, a paver field laid against coping with no restraint there at all tends to creep toward the pool over a season or two, since foot traffic and chairs dragged near the water's edge push outward on exactly that unsupported joint.</p>"),
    ("Setting an Overlay System's Thickness at the Door Threshold",
     "<p>Pavers set over an existing concrete deck add real height, typically the bedding system plus the paver itself, and that buildup has to be checked against every door threshold, pool equipment housing and the coping line before the job starts, not after. We often shave a shallow setback into the old slab near a sliding door, or choose a thinner bedding system specifically at that transition, so the finished paver surface doesn't end up higher than the threshold it meets. On a {city} overlay job, missing that check is how a finished deck ends up funneling rainwater back into the house at exactly the door people use most.</p>"),
    ("Picking a Pattern That Works With Deck Drains and the Coping Curve",
     "<p>A pool deck's pattern has to resolve against a curved coping line and whatever deck drains or trench grates sit in the field, which a straight patio edge rarely has to deal with. Herringbone and running bond both cut cleanly along a gentle curve with some planning, while a tight radius usually calls for more cut pieces near the coping than a rectangular patio would ever need. On a {city} deck, we keep full, uncut pavers in the main walking paths between the house and the water and push the unavoidable cut pieces toward the field's outer edges, away from where feet actually land.</p>"),
    ("Sealing Travertine Isn't Optional the Way It Is on Concrete Pavers",
     "<p>Concrete pavers can go years unsealed with no real problem beyond a faded surface, but travertine's porosity makes sealing closer to a requirement than an option on a deck this close to a pool, where sunscreen, chlorine splash and standing water all have more chances to soak into unsealed stone. A {city} homeowner choosing travertine for its look and its cooler surface in full sun should plan for that sealing schedule as part of the material choice, not as optional maintenance added later. Shell stone carries a similar porosity and benefits from the same approach, even though it's a different material entirely.</p>"),
  ],
  "process": [
    ("Protecting the Pool While the Old Deck Comes Out",
     "<p>Demolishing an existing paver or concrete deck around a pool that's staying filled means covering the water before the first paver or slab section comes up, since broken material and dust land in the pool far more easily than most homeowners expect. We stage removal in sections working away from the coping rather than breaking out the whole field at once, which keeps the open excavation smaller and easier to keep debris out of at any point in a {city} job. Pulling coping units last, right before the new base goes in, also protects the bond beam from impact damage during the rest of the demolition.</p>"),
    ("Running the Pool Through a Paver Deck Install",
     "<p>A pool can usually stay open and chemically maintained through most of a paver deck job, since the work happens around the water rather than in it, as long as the equipment pad and its plumbing stay protected from excavation and compaction equipment nearby. We sequence the base and bedding work around any scheduled filter backwash or pool service visit rather than making the homeowner reschedule theirs. On a {city} project, that coordination means the pool doesn't sit untreated and turning green while the paver field takes shape around it over several days of work.</p>"),
    ("Laying Field Pavers From the Coping Outward",
     "<p>Once the base is compacted and bedding sand is screeded, we typically start the field pattern at the fixed coping line and work outward toward the perimeter restraint, since the coping's curve is the one edge in the layout that can't be adjusted after the fact. Border and cut pieces get set last at the outer edge, where small adjustments in cut width are easier to absorb than they would be right against the pool. On a {city} deck of average size, this stage usually runs a full day or two, depending on how much cutting the coping curve and any deck drains require.</p>"),
    ("Setting Mortar-Set Coping on Its Own Schedule",
     "<p>Coping goes in on a different timeline than the sand-set field pavers around it, since mortar needs its own cure time before the coping units can take weight or be struck with a tool during finishing. We typically set coping early in the sequence, let it cure, then build the field paver work up to that fixed, cured edge rather than trying to finish both systems on the same day. On a {city} job, rushing the field pavers against coping that hasn't fully cured risks knocking a unit out of line before the mortar has set up enough to hold it in place.</p>"),
    ("Scheduling Joint Sand and Sealing Around a Rainy-Season Pour",
     "<p>Joint sand compaction and sealing both want a dry window to set up properly, which matters more on a pool deck job scheduled during the rainy season that typically runs from late May into October across this part of Florida. We try to finish base compaction before midday and push joint sand and sealing to the driest stretch available in the forecast, since a downpour on freshly swept joint sand before it's compacted can wash sand straight toward the pool. On a {city} job, laying the pavers themselves tolerates a light shower far better than the joint-locking stage ever does.</p>"),
    ("Overlay Prep: Cleaning and Testing the Old Slab Before Pavers Go Down",
     "<p>An overlay job starts with testing, not installing, since every square foot of the old concrete deck has to be sound enough to carry a new paver system before bedding material goes on top of it. We sound the slab for hollow spots, pressure-wash it to remove any sealer or coating that would block adhesion, and patch anything that fails before the bedding system goes down. On a {city} overlay, this prep stage often takes as long as the paver-laying stage itself, since a problem found here is far cheaper to fix than one discovered after the new surface is already down.</p>"),
    ("What a Permit Inspection Checks on a Paver Pool Deck",
     "<p>Where {city} or the county requires a permit for the deck work, an inspector typically wants to see the compacted base and the coping or footer layout before pavers go down, similar to how a concrete deck gets checked at the forming stage. That review usually happens once the base is finished but before bedding sand and pavers cover it, so we schedule the inspection at that point rather than waiting until the job looks finished. A failed inspection on the coping detail specifically means pulling pavers already set, which costs real time on a {city} job running on a tight schedule.</p>"),
    ("The Final Walk: Checking Drains, the Coping Joint and Every Loose Paver",
     "<p>With joints swept and compacted, we walk the finished deck checking every deck drain for flow, running a hose along the coping to confirm the flexible sealant joint is sealed end to end, and resetting any paver that rocks or sits proud in its sand bed before calling the job finished. On a {city} deck, this final check with water running catches a slope problem a dry walk-through would miss entirely, since a flat or slightly misgraded section doesn't always look wrong until water is moving across it. Cleanup includes clearing excess joint sand off the coping and out of the pool if any blew in.</p>"),
  ],
  "risks": [
    ("A Paver Field That Slopes the Wrong Way Dumps Debris in the Pool",
     "<p>A paver deck graded flat or tilted toward the water instead of toward its drains sends pollen, cut grass and yard debris straight into the pool with every rain, which throws off the chemical balance faster than ordinary splash-out and evaporation would. On a {city} property, that kind of misgrade usually traces back to a base that was leveled by eye rather than shaped with the pool deck's two-direction slope built in from the start. The pattern often shows up first as an unusually chemical-hungry pool rather than as visibly standing water, since a flat paver field doesn't always look wrong until someone runs a hose across it.</p>"),
    ("No Flexible Joint at the Coping Means a Cracking or Rocking Edge",
     "<p>A paver field butted directly against mortar-set coping with sand instead of a flexible sealant joint eventually shows it, usually as the first course of pavers against the pool starting to rock or the sand joint there opening up faster than anywhere else in the field. The coping and the field move independently whether or not anyone detailed for it, so skipping the flexible joint doesn't prevent that movement, it just leaves nothing absorbing it. A {city} homeowner noticing looseness specifically in the row of pavers closest to the water, and nowhere else in the field, is looking at a missing or failed coping joint.</p>"),
    ("Saltwater Pools Leave Salt Behind on the Coping and Nearby Pavers",
     "<p>A saltwater pool runs at a far lower concentration than seawater, but splash-out and backwash still carry that salt onto the coping and the few feet of paver field closest to the water, and it stays behind as a dull white residue once the water evaporates. It looks similar to ordinary efflorescence on new concrete pavers but comes from the pool itself rather than from the paver material curing, and it keeps recurring on a schedule tied to pool use rather than fading out over a season. On a {city} deck, the coping and splash zone need a routine rinse the rest of the field doesn't.</p>"),
    ("Height Mismatch at the Door When Pavers Go Over an Old Deck",
     "<p>Pavers set over an existing concrete deck add height, and if that buildup wasn't checked against the door thresholds before the job started, the finished surface can end up sitting higher than a slider or French door's sill. That mismatch does more than look wrong, it can let rainwater that used to drain away from the house instead pool against the threshold and work its way inside during a hard storm. A {city} overlay job that skipped this check at the design stage usually needs the threshold itself modified after the fact, a far more disruptive fix than planning for it before the first paver went down.</p>"),
    ("Slick Travertine and Algae Where the Deck Stays Wettest",
     "<p>Honed or polished travertine already has less texture than a textured concrete paver, and in the section of deck closest to the water, or any shaded stretch under a lanai roof, that smoother surface combined with algae film is where slip incidents on a pool deck tend to happen. A {city} homeowner choosing a very smooth travertine finish for its look should weigh that against the splash zone's slip risk specifically, since a textured or tumbled finish holds up better underfoot in exactly the spot that stays wet longest. Regular cleaning reduces the algae side of that risk but doesn't change how slick polished stone gets when wet.</p>"),
    ("Screen Cage Footers Shifting a Paver Field Near the Pool",
     "<p>A screen enclosure footer that wasn't isolated from the surrounding paver base during construction can settle or heave on its own schedule, and the nearest paver courses follow it, showing up as a localized dip or bump that tracks each post location around the pool rather than a random low spot. That pattern is often mistaken for ordinary base settling until someone notices it lines up with every footer rather than spreading evenly across the field. On a {city} deck with an older screen cage, resetting the affected pavers usually fixes the symptom, but the footer itself may need its own separate repair if it keeps moving.</p>"),
    ("Settling Near the Coping Versus Something in the Pool Itself",
     "<p>A low spot or opening joint right along the coping is almost always paver base settling, the base under that stretch losing compaction or washing out from splash-out, rather than any movement in the pool's own structure. True structural issues in a pool shell show up inside the pool's interior finish, not as a dip in the surrounding deck. Orange County carries a higher concentration of documented sinkhole claims statewide; Sarasota and Manatee do not. A {city} homeowner seeing unevenness specifically along the coping line should expect a base and drainage conversation, not a call about the pool shell itself.</p>"),
    ("Irrigation Overspray Washing Fines Out From Under the Coping",
     "<p>A sprinkler head aimed even slightly toward the pool deck soaks the paver base under the coping every cycle, and that repeated, concentrated water washes fines out from underneath the pavers nearest the pool faster than ordinary rain spread evenly across the whole field. Once that erosion starts, the edge restraint holding the coping-adjacent pavers in place loses the compacted base it needs to grip. On a {city} deck, confirming irrigation heads clear the finished paver footprint, especially anywhere near the coping, costs nothing and heads off a repair that otherwise starts small and expands with every watering cycle.</p>"),
  ],
  "care": [
    ("Rinsing the Coping and Splash Zone More Often Than the Rest of the Deck",
     "<p>The coping and the few feet of paver field closest to the water pick up salt, chlorine residue and sunscreen oils far faster than the rest of the deck, which only sees ordinary rain and dust. A quick hose-down of just that zone a few times a month, separate from whatever cleaning schedule the rest of the {city} deck gets, keeps those residues from building into staining a mild cleaner can't fully lift later on. This is less upkeep overall than it sounds, since it's a narrow strip of deck rather than the whole field that needs the extra attention.</p>"),
    ("Checking the Coping's Flexible Joint for Wear",
     "<p>The sealant in the flexible joint between coping and field pavers absorbs the independent movement of the pool and the deck around it, and like any sealant it wears out well before the pavers or the coping itself show any age. A {city} homeowner who checks that joint once or twice a year for cracking, gaps or sections that have pulled free from either surface catches a failing joint while a simple recaulk still solves it. Letting that joint fail quietly lets water and debris work into exactly the gap it was built to protect, a slower problem to notice than it is to fix early.</p>"),
    ("Re-Sanding Joints Near a Pool Needs a Shorter Interval",
     "<p>Joint sand anywhere in a paver field wears thin from rain and foot traffic over time, but the rows closest to a pool lose sand faster still, since splash-out and pool-service hose-downs wash sand out of those joints on top of normal weathering. Topping off and compacting joint sand in that zone on a shorter cycle than the rest of a {city} deck, rather than waiting for the whole field to need it at once, keeps the pavers nearest the water from rocking before the rest of the deck shows any sign of wear at all.</p>"),
    ("Sealing Travertine and Shell Stone on a Pool-Specific Schedule",
     "<p>Travertine and shell stone's porosity means sealing matters more around a pool than it does on a patio further from water, since sunscreen, chlorine splash and standing puddles all have more chances to soak in before a sealer wears through. Rather than resealing on a fixed calendar, which neither material has a published interval for, the better signal on a {city} deck is watching whether water still beads on the surface near the water's edge specifically, since that zone wears through a sealer faster than the rest of the field. A faded or flattened color in that same zone is the other early sign.</p>"),
    ("Cleaning Algae Off a Deck That Never Fully Dries",
     "<p>A paver deck that borders a pool rarely gets a chance to dry out completely, between splash-out, pool-service hose-downs and shade from a screen cage or nearby trees, which makes it more prone to algae growth than an open patio in full sun ever is. A mild cleaner suited to the paver or stone finish, applied with a stiff brush and rinsed well, clears most of it without stripping joint sand or a sealer coat the way a close-range pressure washer does. On a {city} deck, treating the splash zone and any shaded stretch as its own cleaning schedule keeps algae from building faster than the rest of the field shows it.</p>"),
    ("Upkeep on an Overlay Deck Versus a Ground-Up Install",
     "<p>A paver system set over an existing concrete deck needs the same joint and surface care as a ground-up install, but it's worth an occasional check at the overlay's outer edge and at any door threshold, where the bedding system meets the old slab's edge, for signs of the two layers separating. That edge is the weak point in an overlay that a ground-up paver field, with edge restraint anchored into a full compacted base, doesn't really have. On a {city} overlay job, catching an edge that's started to lift early means resetting a border course, not redoing the whole transition.</p>"),
    ("A Seasonal Rinse Timed Before the Rainy Season Starts",
     "<p>A broader rinse and sweep of the whole paver deck a few times a year, timed ahead of the rainy season that typically runs from late May into October in this part of Florida, clears pollen and leaf litter before it works into joints near the deck drains and slows how fast water moves off the surface. On a {city} deck, this routine rinse matters as much for keeping the drains themselves clear as it does for the paver joints, since debris collecting right at a drain inlet defeats the two-direction slope the base was built with in the first place.</p>"),
    ("Watching for Ordinary Wear Versus a Coping Problem",
     "<p>A little sand loss or minor fading across the open part of a paver deck, away from the pool's edge, is ordinary wear that regular re-sanding and the occasional cleaning handle without much thought. What's worth a closer look is wear concentrated specifically at the coping joint or in the row of pavers right against it, since that pattern usually means the flexible sealant or the edge restraint there needs attention rather than a general deck-wide problem. For a {city} homeowner, the practical habit is checking that one zone a little more often than the rest of the deck gets checked.</p>"),
  ],
}
